<%- include('partials/header', { title: title }) %>

<section class="hero">
    <h1>Todo MVC</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">
        Server-rendered CRUD on top of Express 5, TypeScript, MongoDB, and EJS.
    </p>

    <div class="stat-grid">
        <div class="stat">
            <div class="stat-value"><%= counts.total %></div>
            <div class="stat-label">Total</div>
        </div>
        <div class="stat">
            <div class="stat-value"><%= counts.active %></div>
            <div class="stat-label">Active</div>
        </div>
        <div class="stat">
            <div class="stat-value"><%= counts.completed %></div>
            <div class="stat-label">Completed</div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <p class="cta">
        <a class="button primary" href="/todos">View all todos</a>
        <a class="button" href="/todos/new">Add a new todo</a>
    </p>
</section>

<section class="explainer">
    <h2>What is this?</h2>
    <p>
        A tiny CRUD app that renders HTML on the server. Compare it to its
        JSON-API sibling <code>todo-mongo-api-express</code> — same stack, same
        database, but where that project returns JSON at
        <code>/api/todos</code>, this one renders EJS templates and redirects
        after form submits.
    </p>
    <p>
        The controllers, models, and routes are laid out in the classic MVC
        pattern:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>M</strong>odel — <code>src/models/todo.ts</code> (Mongoose schema)</li>
        <li><strong>V</strong>iew — <code>views/todos/*.ejs</code> (EJS templates)</li>
        <li><strong>C</strong>ontroller — <code>src/controllers/todos.ts</code> (renders / redirects)</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<%- include('partials/footer') %>
